1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,930
A key is how you tell rows apart and how you point at them later.

2
00:00:03,930 --> 00:00:09,305
Start with a super key: any set of columns that is unique for every row.

3
00:00:09,305 --> 00:00:13,527
The whole row is a super key if no two rows are identical.

4
00:00:13,527 --> 00:00:15,222
That is a weak compliment.

5
00:00:15,222 --> 00:00:16,777
You want a smaller set.

6
00:00:16,777 --> 00:00:23,222
A candidate key is a super key with no leftover column you could drop and still be unique.

7
00:00:23,222 --> 00:00:27,083
email might be a candidate if you truly never repeat it.

8
00:00:27,083 --> 00:00:30,361
customer_id might be another candidate.

9
00:00:30,361 --> 00:00:33,388
You pick one candidate to be the primary key.

10
00:00:33,388 --> 00:00:35,208
The others can stay UNIQUE.

11
00:00:35,208 --> 00:00:40,111
A good primary key is unique, always present, and stable.

12
00:00:40,111 --> 00:00:44,763
Stable means it does not change because marketing renamed a product code.

13
00:00:44,763 --> 00:00:50,263
Natural keys come from the world: ISBN, email, government id.

14
00:00:50,263 --> 00:00:54,041
They are meaningful, and they change when the world changes.

15
00:00:54,041 --> 00:00:58,791
Surrogate keys are invented: an integer the database assigns.

16
00:00:58,791 --> 00:01:01,222
They are dull, and that is the point.

17
00:01:01,222 --> 00:01:03,625
They do not encode the customer's city.

18
00:01:03,625 --> 00:01:06,416
They do not change when the email changes.

19
00:01:06,416 --> 00:01:10,736
Prefer a surrogate for the primary key when the natural ones wiggle.

20
00:01:10,736 --> 00:01:14,458
Still put UNIQUE on the natural candidates you care about.

21
00:01:14,458 --> 00:01:19,833
ISBN should stay unique even if book_id is the primary key.

22
00:01:19,833 --> 00:01:21,833
Composite keys are allowed.

23
00:01:21,833 --> 00:01:25,444
order_id and line_no together.

24
00:01:25,444 --> 00:01:27,625
They are honest for weak entities.

25
00:01:27,625 --> 00:01:32,013
They are clumsy as foreign keys in every child of the child.

26
00:01:32,013 --> 00:01:36,277
Sometimes a surrogate on the line table is worth the extra column.

27
00:01:36,277 --> 00:01:38,944
Never use a name as a primary key.

28
00:01:38,944 --> 00:01:41,194
Never use a mutable status.

29
00:01:41,194 --> 00:01:46,125
Never use something you would be embarrassed to print on an invoice ten years later.

30
00:01:46,125 --> 00:01:48,250
Write down every candidate.

31
00:01:48,250 --> 00:01:50,333
Strike the ones that can be null.

32
00:01:50,333 --> 00:01:52,361
Strike the ones that change.

33
00:01:52,361 --> 00:01:56,125
Strike the ones that are too wide to copy into children.

34
00:01:56,125 --> 00:01:57,888
Pick the dullest remaining.

35
00:01:57,888 --> 00:02:02,708
That dull integer is how orders will find customers for the rest of the sketch.

36
00:02:02,708 --> 00:02:04,958
UUIDs are surrogates too.

37
00:02:04,958 --> 00:02:08,458
They spread better across shards, and they are wide.

38
00:02:08,458 --> 00:02:11,222
Integers are easier to type while you learn.

39
00:02:11,222 --> 00:02:14,652
Either is dull enough if you do not encode meaning in them.

40
00:02:14,652 --> 00:02:17,013
Do not put the year into the id.

41
00:02:17,013 --> 00:02:19,694
Do not put the table name into the id.

42
00:02:19,694 --> 00:02:21,902
Those facts belong in columns.

43
00:02:21,902 --> 00:02:25,708
Natural keys that look unique globally often are not.

44
00:02:25,708 --> 00:02:27,180
Email changes.

45
00:02:27,180 --> 00:02:29,041
Phone numbers are reused.

46
00:02:29,041 --> 00:02:32,847
National ids are sensitive and not always present.

47
00:02:32,847 --> 00:02:42,375
ISBN is one of the better natural keys, and still needs UNIQUE beside a surrogate if you also sell used copies as inventory rows.

48
00:02:42,375 --> 00:02:45,388
Inventory is a different entity from title.

49
00:02:45,388 --> 00:02:48,347
The key conversation will catch that if you let it.

50
00:02:48,347 --> 00:02:52,361
Composite primary keys make ORM tools grumpy.

51
00:02:52,361 --> 00:02:56,194
That is not a modeling reason, but it is a project reason.

52
00:02:56,194 --> 00:03:00,597
If you add a surrogate to please a tool, keep the composite UNIQUE.

53
00:03:00,597 --> 00:03:03,208
The tool should not erase the candidate.

54
00:03:03,208 --> 00:03:05,694
Document why the primary key won.

55
00:03:05,694 --> 00:03:09,069
A one-line comment in the schema file is enough.

56
00:03:09,069 --> 00:03:13,777
Future you will thank present you when someone wants to key on email again.

57
00:03:13,777 --> 00:03:16,972
Dull, present, stable, unique.

58
00:03:16,972 --> 00:03:19,402
Write those four words above the column.

59
00:03:19,402 --> 00:03:20,416
Then pick.

60
00:03:20,416 --> 00:03:24,444
A key you are proud of is often a key that encodes a story.

61
00:03:24,444 --> 00:03:25,902
Stories change.

62
00:03:25,902 --> 00:03:27,333
Dull keys do not.

63
00:03:27,333 --> 00:03:31,819
If you can say the key out loud as a sentence about the business, look again.

64
00:03:31,819 --> 00:03:35,944
customer_id 104 is not a sentence.

65
00:03:35,944 --> 00:03:41,500
AUSTIN-MAYA-2024 is a sentence, and a trap.

66
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:43,486
Keep the story in columns.

67
00:03:43,486 --> 00:03:45,555
Keep the key empty of plot.

68
00:03:45,555 --> 00:03:48,347
UNIQUE the story if it must stay unique.

69
00:03:48,347 --> 00:03:50,208
Point children at the dull one.

70
00:03:50,208 --> 00:03:53,277
That split will still look wise in five years.

71
00:03:53,277 --> 00:03:58,027
Write it into the schema comments so nobody 'improves' the id later.

72
00:03:58,027 --> 00:04:01,194
Improvement is how encoded keys are born.

73
00:04:01,194 --> 00:04:02,875
Refuse the improvement.

74
00:04:02,875 --> 00:04:04,402
Pick dull, and stop.

75
00:04:04,402 --> 00:04:06,027
People change names.

76
00:04:06,027 --> 00:04:07,513
They share birthdays.

77
00:04:07,513 --> 00:04:14,361
Systems that treated name plus date of birth as identity have merged two humans into one record.

78
00:04:14,361 --> 00:04:23,333
A primary key is one or more attributes that identify a row, unique, and never a second meaning hiding in the same columns.

79
00:04:23,333 --> 00:04:26,444
A table has exactly one primary key.

80
00:04:26,444 --> 00:04:32,736
A super key is any set of columns that is still unique, including extras you do not need.

81
00:04:32,736 --> 00:04:37,236
email plus city might still be unique because email already was.

82
00:04:37,236 --> 00:04:39,000
The city is surplus.

83
00:04:39,122 --> 00:04:46,127
A candidate key is a super key with no surplus: take anything away and uniqueness dies.

84
00:04:46,127 --> 00:04:48,099
email might be a candidate.

85
00:04:48,099 --> 00:04:49,863
username might be another.

86
00:04:49,863 --> 00:04:52,891
You pick one candidate to be the primary key.

87
00:04:52,891 --> 00:04:55,335
The others can be UNIQUE constraints.

88
00:04:55,335 --> 00:05:05,002
The process is: list candidates from real data, pick the stable dull one, or invent a surrogate if none of the candidates will stay still.

89
00:05:05,002 --> 00:05:10,849
Natural keys come from the world: ISBN, national identifiers, email.

90
00:05:10,849 --> 00:05:13,474
They are tempting because they look meaningful.

91
00:05:13,474 --> 00:05:19,572
They are dangerous when the world reissues them, recycles them, or lets people change them.

92
00:05:19,572 --> 00:05:26,363
A surrogate is an attribute you create, often an integer, that means only: this row.

93
00:05:26,363 --> 00:05:31,238
customer_id one hundred four is not a sentence about the business.

94
00:05:31,238 --> 00:05:42,044
AUSTIN-MAYA-2024 is a sentence, and a trap, because Austin, Maya, and 2024 can all change their jobs in that code.

95
00:05:42,044 --> 00:05:43,863
Keep story in columns.

96
00:05:43,863 --> 00:05:46,599
Keep the primary key empty of plot.

97
00:05:46,599 --> 00:05:54,780
Composite keys are honest when identity is honestly several parts: order_id plus line_number.

98
00:05:54,780 --> 00:05:59,808
They are painful as parents, because every child must copy every part.

99
00:05:59,808 --> 00:06:04,002
If many tables will point here, a small surrogate is kinder.

100
00:06:04,002 --> 00:06:12,947
Never use a value people type from memory as the only identity if a typo would attach medical or money rows to the wrong person.

101
00:06:12,947 --> 00:06:15,127
Never pick a key you will want to edit.

102
00:06:15,127 --> 00:06:17,294
Primary keys are referenced.

103
00:06:17,294 --> 00:06:21,405
Edits cascade into every child or they are refused.

104
00:06:21,405 --> 00:06:28,599
If you must expose a public code, make it UNIQUE and still keep a dull internal id as the primary key.

105
00:06:28,599 --> 00:06:30,599
Children point at the dull one.

106
00:06:30,599 --> 00:06:32,738
Humans search on the public one.

107
00:06:32,738 --> 00:06:35,974
Look at sample data before you declare uniqueness.

108
00:06:35,974 --> 00:06:41,294
Two rows that look unique in a spreadsheet of twenty can collide at twenty thousand.

109
00:06:41,294 --> 00:06:48,030
If no candidate survives contact with growth, create the new attribute and make it the primary key.

110
00:06:48,030 --> 00:06:50,155
That is not a failure of modeling.

111
00:06:50,155 --> 00:06:54,752
That is how most operational tables stay identifyable for a decade.

112
00:06:54,752 --> 00:07:03,488
Write the choice down: this is the primary key, these are the unique candidates, this is why the meaningful code is not the pointer.

113
00:07:03,488 --> 00:07:08,210
Then refuse the later improvement that encodes a city into the id.

114
00:07:08,210 --> 00:07:11,377
Improvement is how encoded keys are born.

115
00:07:11,377 --> 00:07:14,210
Pick keys for five store tables in order.

116
00:07:14,210 --> 00:07:20,280
users: candidate email, candidate username, surrogate user_id.

117
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:28,919
Email changes, username might be recycled, so user_id primary, UNIQUE email, UNIQUE username.

118
00:07:28,919 --> 00:07:36,974
products: SKU looks natural and is reused by vendors, so product_id surrogate, UNIQUE sku.

119
00:07:36,974 --> 00:07:41,544
orders: order_id surrogate, not a date stamp code.

120
00:07:41,544 --> 00:07:54,058
reviews: review_id surrogate, because the same user can review twice if the business allows, or UNIQUE (user_id, product_id) if it does not.

121
00:07:54,058 --> 00:08:05,016
order_lines: composite (order_id, line_no) is honest, or line_id surrogate if fulfillment talks in global ids.

122
00:08:05,016 --> 00:08:11,516
Super keys would include user_id plus email; that extra email is surplus.

123
00:08:11,516 --> 00:08:14,919
Candidate keys are the minimal unique sets.

124
00:08:14,919 --> 00:08:17,391
Primary is the candidate you point at.

125
00:08:17,391 --> 00:08:22,155
The hospital parable stays in your ear: do not pick name plus birth date.

126
00:08:22,155 --> 00:08:24,433
Look at sample rows that collide.

127
00:08:24,433 --> 00:08:25,585
Then pick dull.

128
00:08:25,585 --> 00:08:29,224
Close with questions, the way a careful lecture does.

129
00:08:29,224 --> 00:08:32,280
Answer in a sentence you could defend at a keyboard.

130
00:08:32,280 --> 00:08:33,947
What is a primary key?

131
00:08:33,947 --> 00:08:39,127
The candidate you pick to identify the row, unique, one per table.

132
00:08:39,127 --> 00:08:40,183
Super key?

133
00:08:40,183 --> 00:08:43,794
Any unique set, including surplus columns.

134
00:08:43,794 --> 00:08:44,988
Candidate key?

135
00:08:44,988 --> 00:08:46,724
A minimal unique set.

136
00:08:46,724 --> 00:08:48,474
Natural versus surrogate?

137
00:08:48,474 --> 00:08:51,655
From the world versus invented as this-row.

138
00:08:51,655 --> 00:08:53,085
Why dull ids?

139
00:08:53,085 --> 00:08:55,877
They do not encode a story that will change.

140
00:08:55,877 --> 00:08:57,363
Name plus birth date?

141
00:08:57,363 --> 00:08:59,766
A merging disaster waiting to happen.

142
00:08:59,766 --> 00:09:01,627
Email as primary key?

143
00:09:01,627 --> 00:09:04,724
Usually a unique handle, not the pointer.

144
00:09:04,724 --> 00:09:06,322
Composite as parent?

145
00:09:06,322 --> 00:09:10,974
Every child copies every part; often a surrogate is kinder.

146
00:09:10,974 --> 00:09:12,391
Look at sample data?

147
00:09:12,391 --> 00:09:15,919
Collisions appear at scale, not at twenty rows.

148
00:09:15,919 --> 00:09:18,377
Public code plus internal id?

149
00:09:18,377 --> 00:09:21,669
UNIQUE the code, point children at the id.

150
00:09:21,669 --> 00:09:26,391
If an answer needed a second table, go draw it before you write more SQL.

